<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estrada Doctrine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estrada Doctrine was a principle of Mexican foreign policy set out in 1930 by Foreign Secretary Genaro Estrada. It held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> would not grant or withhold formal recognition of foreign governments, since that meant judging their legitimacy and interfering in other states' affairs, but would simply keep or withdraw its diplomats. It became the basis of Mexico's long policy of non-intervention.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Genaro Estrada was Secretary of Foreign Affairs under President Pascual Ortiz Rubio (1930–1932), and the doctrine is also known as La Doctrina Mexicana or La Doctrina Ortiz Rubio. Estrada set it out on 27 September 1930: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> would keep or withdraw its diplomatic agents, and continue to accept those of other nations, as it saw fit, without judging, hastily or afterwards, the right of nations to accept, keep or replace their governments.</p><p>It came as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was regaining its place abroad. Problems with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> had kept it out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, which admitted it on 12 September 1931, and it returned to the International Conference of American States, from which it had been excluded because the United States did not recognise its government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The doctrine treats the formal recognition of de facto governments as a degrading practice: by judging the legality and legitimacy of another government, a state intrudes on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and leaves it open to outside judgement of its internal affairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> had itself suffered from the practice, having found it hard to win recognition of its independence. The doctrine rests on the principles of non-intervention, the peaceful settlement of disputes and the self-determination of nations. Its widest use came in the 1970s, when Mexico withdrew recognition from none of the South American governments formed by coups; its only measure against them was to withdraw its diplomatic mission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Critics asked whether it was morally and politically right for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to stay neutral towards governments they regarded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a>. In 1987 Jorge Castañeda Gutman called the appeal to principles over interests a diplomatic hypocrisy. As Secretary of Foreign Affairs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-fox">Vicente Fox</a> (2000–2006), he and his successor Luis Ernesto Derbez tried to set the doctrine aside, a shift called the Castañeda Doctrine. Under President Andrés Manuel López Obrador Mexico returned to it, notably during the 2019 Venezuelan presidential crisis, and in 2026 it cited the doctrine to keep its distance from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> intervention in Venezuela.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estrada Doctrine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mexico gave asylum to Spanish Republicans after 1939 and to Leon Trotsky, and its foreign policy of non-intervention, set out in the <b>Estrada Doctrine</b> of 1930, made it an independent voice during the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
